{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>Keep the day, etc.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 27a) that זָכוֹר and שָׁמוֹר were spoken in a single utterance. According to their words, it is possible to say that His saying <b>as He commanded you</b> was to remind them about שָׁמוֹר, for they were also commanded concerning it from the mouth of HaShem on the day of the assembly. And the intention of שָׁמוֹר, they of blessed memory said (Mekhilta, on the verse זָכוֹר), is to guard against following after it [i.e., weekday pursuits]. Now one should know why שָׁמוֹר was not said there together with זָכוֹר. Perhaps the intention was to separate them, so that one would not think that the utterance שָׁמוֹר refers to the time that follows upon the utterance זָכוֹר which precedes it — in this manner, that one should remember in order to keep the Sabbath — and by separating them He indicated that they are two commandments, one at the entrance [of the Sabbath] and one at the exit. Or perhaps it is because HaShem never said anything but זָכוֹר, except that the one [i.e., שָׁמוֹר] is heard from its companion by those who understand, and when HaShem said זָכוֹר, the intention was that one should remember by directing one's mind upon it so that it would not be a rote matter for him, and from the implication of the matter you learn that guarding against following after it is also a commandment — and this is what Moses explained by saying שָׁמוֹר, that it too is included in זָכוֹר. And this I have written by way of possibility."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:26.611129308Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}